1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,773
Diya has a fever.

2
00:00:01,773 --> 00:00:05,316
Her mother holds a digital
thermometer under her tongue.

3
00:00:05,316 --> 00:00:06,216
Beep.

4
00:00:05,869 --> 00:00:07,942
Thirty eight point one degrees.

5
00:00:08,292 --> 00:00:11,068
No mercury, no squinting at a glass tube.

6
00:00:11,068 --> 00:00:14,345
How does a tiny stick read
her temperature so fast?

7
00:00:14,845 --> 00:00:17,752
Inside it is a small chain of electronics.

8
00:00:18,102 --> 00:00:22,856
A sensor, called a thermistor,
changes its resistance as it warms up.

9
00:00:22,856 --> 00:00:24,543
That changes a voltage.

10
00:00:24,893 --> 00:00:27,752
A converter turns that
voltage into numbers.

11
00:00:27,752 --> 00:00:30,550
A microcontroller works
out the temperature.

12
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:32,602
And the display shows it.

13
00:00:32,602 --> 00:00:34,190
Sense, process, show.

14
00:00:34,190 --> 00:00:37,149
Electronics is quietly
doing this all around us.

15
00:00:37,799 --> 00:00:41,296
Let's walk through a home and
spot the electronic blocks.

16
00:00:41,646 --> 00:00:46,024
The phone charger takes two hundred
and thirty volts AC from the wall,

17
00:00:46,024 --> 00:00:50,113
and turns it into about five
volts of steady DC for the phone.

18
00:00:50,463 --> 00:00:55,262
The washing machine has a microcontroller
that runs each step, fill, wash,

19
00:00:55,262 --> 00:00:57,334
rinse and spin, on a timer.

20
00:00:57,684 --> 00:01:01,595
The fridge and the air conditioner
use sensors to switch the compressor

21
00:01:01,595 --> 00:01:04,339
on and off, and keep
the temperature steady.

22
00:01:04,989 --> 00:01:07,313
Now the TV remote, on the breadboard.

23
00:01:07,663 --> 00:01:08,897
Press a button.

24
00:01:08,897 --> 00:01:13,529
The microcontroller inside sends a
code, a pattern of short on and off

25
00:01:13,529 --> 00:01:14,429
pulses.

26
00:01:14,570 --> 00:01:16,813
An infrared light flashes that pattern.

27
00:01:16,813 --> 00:01:19,346
It is light, but our eyes cannot see it.

28
00:01:19,696 --> 00:01:20,923
Watch the scope.

29
00:01:20,923 --> 00:01:24,017
The top trace is the
code sent by the remote.

30
00:01:24,017 --> 00:01:27,410
The bottom trace is what
the TV's receiver picks up.

31
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:29,410
The two patterns match.

32
00:01:29,410 --> 00:01:33,154
The TV reads the code, and
the volume goes up by one.

33
00:01:33,804 --> 00:01:36,395
A different button sends
a different pattern.

34
00:01:36,395 --> 00:01:38,454
That is all a remote really does.

35
00:01:38,454 --> 00:01:40,282
It talks in flashes of light.

36
00:01:40,932 --> 00:01:43,218
Every one of these runs on energy.

37
00:01:43,218 --> 00:01:45,754
So how long does a phone battery last?

38
00:01:46,104 --> 00:01:49,492
A battery's capacity is
written in milliampere hours.

39
00:01:49,842 --> 00:01:52,105
That unit is current times time.

40
00:01:52,105 --> 00:01:54,419
And current times time is charge.

41
00:01:54,419 --> 00:01:58,474
So capacity tells us how much
charge the battery can deliver.

42
00:01:59,174 --> 00:02:04,522
If the phone draws a steady current I,
the battery lasts for a time t equal

43
00:02:04,522 --> 00:02:06,503
to capacity divided by I.

44
00:02:07,203 --> 00:02:08,357
Worked example.

45
00:02:08,357 --> 00:02:12,109
A phone battery is rated four
thousand milliampere hours.

46
00:02:12,109 --> 00:02:16,335
On average, the phone draws two
hundred and fifty milliamperes.

47
00:02:16,685 --> 00:02:20,749
t equals four thousand divided
by two hundred and fifty.

48
00:02:21,449 --> 00:02:23,019
That is sixteen hours.

49
00:02:23,019 --> 00:02:26,820
The milliamperes cancel, so
the answer comes out in hours.

50
00:02:27,470 --> 00:02:29,900
So what has electronics changed for us?

51
00:02:30,250 --> 00:02:32,729
We talk face to face across the world.

52
00:02:32,729 --> 00:02:34,190
We pay with a phone.

53
00:02:34,190 --> 00:02:35,349
We learn online.

54
00:02:35,699 --> 00:02:39,854
Hospitals watch patients every
second, and alarms keep homes safe.

55
00:02:40,504 --> 00:02:42,012
But there is another side.

56
00:02:42,362 --> 00:02:47,629
Old gadgets become electronic waste,
with harmful metals like lead inside.

57
00:02:47,979 --> 00:02:51,430
Too much screen time strains
our eyes and our sleep.

58
00:02:51,430 --> 00:02:53,700
And online fraud is a real risk.

59
00:02:54,350 --> 00:02:56,517
Electronics makes life easier.

60
00:02:56,517 --> 00:02:58,651
Using it wisely is our job.

61
00:02:59,451 --> 00:03:00,917
Now, the exam trap.

62
00:03:01,267 --> 00:03:04,647
Many students think milliampere
hours measure current.

63
00:03:04,647 --> 00:03:05,680
They do not.

64
00:03:06,030 --> 00:03:08,316
Milliampere hours measure charge.

65
00:03:08,316 --> 00:03:09,791
Current times time.

66
00:03:09,791 --> 00:03:13,188
One milliampere hour is
three point six coulombs.

67
00:03:13,838 --> 00:03:15,519
Where is this asked?

68
00:03:15,519 --> 00:03:17,199
In PUC board papers.

69
00:03:17,199 --> 00:03:22,974
Explain the impact of electronics on daily
life, with advantages and disadvantages,

70
00:03:22,974 --> 00:03:26,573
or name the electronic
parts of a common device.

71
00:03:27,223 --> 00:03:28,123
Try this.

72
00:03:28,065 --> 00:03:31,855
A phone battery is rated three
thousand milliampere hours.

73
00:03:31,855 --> 00:03:34,968
How much charge can it
deliver, in coulombs?

74
00:03:40,368 --> 00:03:41,268
B.

75
00:03:40,808 --> 00:03:43,494
Ten thousand, eight hundred coulombs.

76
00:03:43,844 --> 00:03:47,450
Three thousand milliampere
hours is three ampere hours.

77
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:50,823
One hour is three thousand
six hundred seconds.

78
00:03:51,173 --> 00:03:55,578
So charge equals three amperes times
three thousand six hundred seconds,

79
00:03:55,578 --> 00:03:58,645
which is ten thousand,
eight hundred coulombs.

80
00:03:59,295 --> 00:04:00,817
Screenshot this card.

81
00:04:04,017 --> 00:04:08,156
Next, electric charge, current,
voltage and potential difference.

82
00:04:08,156 --> 00:04:11,029
What really pushes
electrons around a circuit?
